Got it — you need the actual SQLAlchemy ORM models for the Inflow/Outflow business service, mapped to your IBM Db2 `JOBS` and `JOB_ITEMS` tables.

The models should represent:

- `Job`: one parent job, common input, overall status, and timestamps.
- `JobItem`: one portfolio calculation, its input/output, status, retry count, and a relationship to its parent job.

I'll use SQLAlchemy 2.x-style declarative models. The database connection must use a Db2-compatible SQLAlchemy dialect and driver configured in your environment.

## 1. `models/job.py`

```
from sqlalchemy import (    BigInteger,    CheckConstraint,    CLOB,    Column,    ForeignKey,    Identity,    Index,    Integer,    String,    TIMESTAMP,    Text,    UniqueConstraint,    text,)from sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base, relationshipBase = declarative_base()class Job(Base):    __tablename__ = "JOBS"    job_id = Column(        BigInteger,        Identity(always=True),        primary_key=True,    )    public_job_id = Column(        String(64),        nullable=False,        unique=True,    )    job_type = Column(String(100), nullable=False)    status = Column(        String(20),        nullable=False,        server_default=text("'QUEUED'"),    )    input_payload = Column(CLOB(2 * 1024 * 1024))    created_by = Column(String(255))    idempotency_key = Column(String(255))    created_at = Column(        TIMESTAMP,        nullable=False,        server_default=text("CURRENT TIMESTAMP"),    )    started_at = Column(TIMESTAMP)    completed_at = Column(TIMESTAMP)    items = relationship(        "JobItem",        back_populates="job",        cascade="all, delete-orphan",        passive_deletes=True,        order_by="JobItem.item_index",    )    __table_args__ = (        CheckConstraint(            """            STATUS IN (                'QUEUED', 'RUNNING', 'COMPLETED',                'PARTIAL_FAILED', 'FAILED', 'CANCELLED'            )            """,            name="CK_JOBS_STATUS",        ),        Index(            "IDX_JOBS_STATUS_CREATED",            "status",            "created_at",        ),    )
```

## 2. `models/job_item.py`

```
from sqlalchemy import (    item_index = Column(Integer, nullable=False)    portfolio_id = Column(String(255), nullable=False)    status = Column(        String(20),        nullable=False,        server_default=text("'QUEUED'"),    )    input_payload = Column(CLOB(1024 * 1024))    result_payload = Column(CLOB(2 * 1024 * 1024))    attempts = Column(        Integer,        nullable=False,        server_default=text("0"),    )    max_attempts = Column(        Integer,        nullable=False,        server_default=text("3"),    )    created_at = Column(        TIMESTAMP,        nullable=False,        server_default=text("CURRENT TIMESTAMP"),    )    started_at = Column(TIMESTAMP)    completed_at = Column(TIMESTAMP)    job = relationship(        "Job",        back_populates="items",    )    __table_args__ = (        UniqueConstraint(            "job_id",            "item_index",            name="UQ_JOB_ITEMS_INDEX",        ),        CheckConstraint(            """            STATUS IN (                'QUEUED', 'RUNNING', 'RETRY_WAIT',                'COMPLETED', 'FAILED', 'CANCELLED'            )            """,            name="CK_JOB_ITEMS_STATUS",        ),        CheckConstraint(            "ATTEMPTS >= 0 AND MAX_ATTEMPTS >= 1",            name="CK_JOB_ITEMS_ATTEMPTS",        ),        Index(            "IDX_JOB_ITEMS_PARENT",            "job_id",            "item_index",        ),        Index(            "IDX_JOB_ITEMS_QUEUED",            "job_id",            "status",        ),    )
```

## 3. Example relationship usage

Create one parent job and multiple portfolio items using SQLAlchemy:

```
import jsonimport uuidfrom models.job import Jobfrom models.job_item import JobItemdef create_job(session, common_input, portfolios, created_by):    job = Job(        public_job_id=str(uuid.uuid4()),        job_type="PORTFOLIO_CALCULATION",        input_payload=json.dumps(common_input),        created_by=created_by,    )    for index, portfolio in enumerate(portfolios):        job.items.append(            JobItem(                item_index=index,                portfolio_id=portfolio["portfolio_id"],                input_payload=json.dumps(                    portfolio.get("input", {})                ),            )        )    session.add(job)    session.commit()    return job
```

SQLAlchemy manages the parent-child relationship and persists both the `JOBS` row and its `JOB_ITEMS` rows in the same transaction.

